description:
Add the schema and data access layer for per-user wrapped DEKs.

## Migration (next number after 0013)

  CREATE TABLE user_encryption_keys (
      user_id        BIGINT PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
      wrapped_dek    BYTEA  NOT NULL,
      kms_key_id     TEXT   NOT NULL,   -- ARN of the KMS key used to wrap
      algorithm      TEXT   NOT NULL DEFAULT 'AES_256',
      created_at     BIGINT NOT NULL,
      rotated_at     BIGINT           -- NULL until first rotation
  );

  ON DELETE CASCADE means deleting a user row automatically deletes their
  wrapped DEK row — the foreign key enforces cryptographic erasure.

## UserKeyStore (crates/mail/src/crypto/user_key_store.rs)

  pub struct UserKeyStore {
      pool: Arc<AnyPool>,
      key_service: Arc<dyn KeyService>,
  }

  impl UserKeyStore {
      /// Get or create the plaintext DEK for a user.
      /// On first call: generates a fresh 32-byte DEK, wraps it, stores wrapped version.
      /// On subsequent calls: fetches wrapped DEK from DB, unwraps via key_service.
      /// Returns plaintext DEK valid for the duration of the request.
      /// NEVER caches the plaintext DEK beyond a single call.
      pub async fn get_dek(&self, user_id: i64) -> Result<[u8; 32], UserKeyError>;

      /// Rotate the DEK: generate new DEK, re-encrypt all user data, store new wrapped DEK.
      /// Transactional: if re-encryption fails, old DEK is preserved.
      pub async fn rotate_dek(&self, user_id: i64) -> Result<(), UserKeyError>;

      /// Destroy the DEK permanently. All encrypted data becomes unreadable.
      /// Called on user deletion. Non-reversible.
      pub async fn destroy_dek(&self, user_id: i64) -> Result<(), UserKeyError>;
  }

## Critical invariant: no plaintext DEK persistence
  The plaintext DEK must exist in memory only for the duration of a single
  request. It must never be written to a log, a tracing field, a database
  column, or any durable storage. Add a SAFETY comment at every site where
  the DEK is held in a local variable.

## Acceptance
  - Migration runs on both SQLite and Postgres backends
  - get_dek called twice for the same user_id produces the same plaintext DEK
  - destroy_dek followed by get_dek returns an error (no row to unwrap)
  - ON DELETE CASCADE verified: deleting from users table removes user_encryption_keys row
